#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_sign

#% text
# `function_sign(f, x)` gives `1` when `f >= 0` for every `x` of its domain, `-1` when `f <= 0`, `0` when `f == 0`, and `indeterminate` otherwise.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The sign is proved from the facts of the domain where it can be (`refine`); otherwise, for one variable without parameters, it is found by sampling the domain: a change of sign among the samples gives `indeterminate`.

#% item
# `function_sign([f, cond], x)` restricts the domain.

#% item
# `function_sign(f, n, integers)` for a sequence.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# An absolute value and a sine:

#% cell
function_sign(abs(x), x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_sign(sin(x), x)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A shifted sine:

#% cell
function_sign(sin(x) + 3/2, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# On a restricted domain:

#% cell
function_sign([sin(x), 0 <= x and x <= pi], x)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A difference that is never positive:

#% cell
function_sign(x - sqrt(x^2), x)
#% output
#= -1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `function_monotonicity` is the sign of the derivative:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(x^3, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_sign(d(x^3, x), x)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Without a proof the answer comes from samples of the domain; a sign change between samples very close together can be missed:

#% cell
function_sign(x^2 - 10^-8, x)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_monotonicity`, `function_convexity`, `refine`, `sign`
